  'note.ferrata': 'For experienced hikers; a helmet, harness and cables are recommended.',
  'note.bobijasko': 'Best after snowmelt or heavy rain.',
  'note.cvjetna': 'The Cvjetna trail, about 15 km, links Kamengrad, the waterfall and Jasice.',

  // history
  'history.eyebrow': 'History',
  'history.name.title': 'How the town got its name',
  'history.name.html': 'The name comes from the river Sana, from the Latin <em>sana</em> (“healthy”), as in <em>Aqua Sana</em>. In 1717, in the Ottoman period, the fortress town is mentioned as <em>Džisr-i Sana</em> (<em>džisr</em> = bridge).',
  'history.more': 'The whole history, in eight chapters',
  'history.centuries': 'Through the centuries',
  'history.word': 'Latin · “healthy”',
  'history.chain.river': 'the river',
  'history.chain.today': 'today',
  'tl.1.yr': '6th to 3rd c. BC',
  'tl.1': 'An Iron Age settlement with a necropolis of 176 graves.',
  'tl.2': 'Kamengrad is first mentioned, then held by the Babonić Blagajski princes; the area belongs to the Sana county (župa).',
  'tl.3': 'The Ottomans take the area. Tradition says Sultan Mehmed the Conqueror prayed in the open below Kamengrad; a musalla was made on that spot.',
  'tl.4.yr': '16th c.',
  'tl.4': 'The Hamzibeg Mosque is built (before 1557).',
  'tl.5': 'The Austro-Hungarian army enters Sanski Most on 8 September without a fight. The town becomes a district seat and begins to grow on the right bank of the Sana.',
  'tl.6': 'The Second Session of ZAVNOBiH is held here; it adopts the Declaration on the Rights of Citizens of Bosnia and Herzegovina.',
  'tl.7': 'After the Army of Republika Srpska takes control of the municipality, non-Serb civilians are killed and held in camps; the killed residents are later found in about 30 mass graves. On 31 May, civilians from the village of Begići are killed on the Vrhpolje bridge; in June the Hamzibeg Mosque is demolished.',
  'tl.8': 'The Bosnian Army enters the town in Operation Sana 95, on 9 and 10 October. Sanski Most marks 10 October as Liberation Day and Municipality Day.',
  'tl.9': 'The Hamzibeg Mosque reopens after reconstruction.',
  'tl.f.mulez': 'Mulež',
  'tl.f.hamz': 'The Hamzibeg Mosque today',
  'tl.f.zav': 'The Sokolski dom building, in a book',
  'tl.17': 'In September a via ferrata opens on Mulež, on a rock wall 90 m high.',
  'tl.16': 'The Sana floods the town again; people get through the streets by boat.',
  'tl.15': 'In the census the municipality has 62,102 people, the tenth largest of the hundred municipalities in Bosnia and Herzegovina.',
  'tl.14': 'The townspeople and the municipality build the Sokolski dom, and a new concrete town bridge already crosses the Sana.',
  'tl.13': 'NK Sana is founded. The first football was brought to town in 1913 by Branko Juzbašić, a student in Prague.',
  'tl.12': 'A charter of King Béla IV is the oldest known document that names the Sana, together with the counties of Dubica, Mren and Vrbaška.',
  'tl.11': 'The Maezaei, an Illyrian people, live in this area. The Romans conquer them and set up a mining district between the Sana and the Japra.',
  'tl.11.yr': 'Antiquity',
  'tl.10': 'People of the Vučedol culture live in Hrustovača cave. The finds are distinctive enough that archaeologists speak of a separate Hrustovo type of the culture.',
  'tl.10.yr': 'c. 2600 BC',

